Red-to-green permission regressions likewise reject a write-attempt journal
or checkpoint made group- or world-writable after its initial private atomic
save. Such files cannot be used to confirm an ambiguous attempt, clear its
journal or advance a quiz, and are preserved byte-for-byte for human recovery.
Normal single-link owner-controlled files continue to work without migration.

The canonical persisted executor also has two synthetic integration checks
for aliased recovery files. A hard-linked attempting journal stops at
`attempt-load`; a hard-linked question checkpoint stops at `checkpoint-load`.
Neither path contacts the browser session, acquires pacing nor makes a remote
write, and neither removes or rewrites the aliased bytes. This is portable
recovery evidence, not a live provider receipt.

A red-to-green hard-link regression exposed mutable aliases in both write
attempt journals and saved publication checkpoints. An alternate filename for
the same inode could previously bypass cooperative file locks while the
canonical reader still admitted the record. The adapters now refuse multiple
hard links and verify the opened descriptor's identity, size and metadata
before accepting its content.

Synthetic integration fixtures confirm that an
aliased attempting journal cannot be confirmed or cleared, and an aliased
checkpoint cannot advance a remote question. The original bytes and aliases
remain untouched; historical single-link files still decode normally.

End-to-end persisted-executor fixtures now inject a duplicate phase in an
attempting journal or a duplicate remote set ID in a checkpoint before resume.
Recovery reports a stable file-load failure without querying the browser,
acquiring a pacing lease or issuing a second remote write. The original
uncertain files survive byte-for-byte for manual reconciliation.

A red-to-green recovery regression found that an idempotent checkpoint save
could report success for a different `remoteSetId` at the same operation index.
The same file adapter also accepted advancing later questions under a newly
substituted set ID. It now refuses remote identity changes once Create Set has
been durably confirmed, without replacing the checkpoint or its backup.

Current write-attempt v3 and checkpoint v2 files now require exactly the bytes
produced by their atomic writers. Duplicated phase, index or set fields,
reordered root objects and padded JSON cannot reinterpret publication progress
or authorize cleanup of ambiguous attempts. Legacy attempt v1/v2 and checkpoint
v1 compact or indented inputs retain compatible migrations, but duplicate keys
are refused. Synthetic tests cover successful historical decoding as well as
malformed input, persistent immutability and successful original-ID progress.

The authenticated local data root was inspected only for schema/byte format:
its twelve attempt journals are canonical v3 and its four checkpoints are
canonical v2. No real journal, checkpoint or Blooket content was modified.
These portable proofs do not establish live publication acceptance.

The canonical `questions.list` read-back path now tolerates transient
